<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Umma Khan V</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1761">1761</a>–<a href="/years/1801">1801</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Umma Khan the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a> Khan V, called the Great, was khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> in mountainous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> from 1774 until his death in 1801, when the khanate was at the height of its power. He led raids deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and Shirvan and received tribute and payments from neighbouring rulers, including the kings of Kartli-Kakheti. He also dealt with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, which was then advancing into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umma-khan-v.jpg" alt="Umma Khan V" width="320" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Halil-Bey Mussaijassul · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khalil-Bek_Musayasul._Umma_Khan_of_Avary_hosting_the_Georgian_prince_Alexander.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Umma Khan, also known as Omar Khan and nicknamed the Great or the Mad, was born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khunzakh">Khunzakh</a>, the son of Muhammad IV, khan of the Avars, and of Bakha, daughter of Ahmad Khan, utsmi of the Kaitags. His father, a rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatali-khan-of-quba">Fatali Khan of Quba</a>, marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a> in 1774 and was killed by the Akushans, Dargin allies of Fatali Khan, and Umma inherited the rule of the khanate, as nutsal, at the age of 12.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In his first year he built a coalition of Dagestani rulers against Fatali Khan, marrying his sister Bakhtika to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-khalil-khan">Ibrahim Khalil Khan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a>, and in July 1774 the coalition defeated Fatali's army of 8,000 at Gavdushan, near Khudat. Urged on by the Ottoman sultan and the pasha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhaltsikhe">Akhaltsikhe</a>, he entered the Alazan valley in September 1784 with an army of 15,000, outmanoeuvred Heraclius II and a Russian detachment, and raided Kartli, Lori and Upper Imereti. At the fortress of Vakhani 700 people were captured and all the men except the princes put to death. Heraclius agreed to pay him 10,000 rubles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> a year.</p><p>At the end of 1786 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a>, burned it and killed its inhabitants after Fatali Khan failed to pay what he had promised, and forced him to hand over the revenues of Salyan and 200,000 rubles. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-khalil-khan">Ibrahim Khalil Khan</a> he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhchivan</a> after a 17-day siege, but the two were defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoy">Khoy</a> in 1788–1789. When Russian troops entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in 1796 the payments stopped.In 1799 the Georgian prince Alexander asked his help to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a>; on 7 November 1800 a Georgian and Russian force defeated them at the Battle of Niakhura, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> lost 2,000 men and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a> was severely injured.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Umma Khan was preparing a campaign against the Ganja Khanate when he fell ill and died in Balaken on 22 March 1801. Abbasgulu Bakikhanov held that he died of wounds from the battle on the Iori, and Genichutlinsky reported a rumour that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javad-khan">Javad Khan</a> of Ganja had him poisoned. He had only daughters, and his half-brother Gebek, who succeeded him, was later murdered by Umma's widow Gihilay, ending the dynasty of the nutsals. During his reign Dibirqadi al-Khunzakhi developed a 38-letter alphabet in Arabic script for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> language, and in 1796 Umma banned certain pagan practices.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Umma Khan V” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Umma Khan</b>, who ruled from 1774, led raids deep into Georgia and Shirvan, and the khanate received subsidies from the Safavid Empire and later Russia.</blockquote><a href="/state/avar-khanate">Avar Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Khunzakh, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in Balakan, in what is now <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.55,46.71&amp;year=1787" aria-label="Khunzakh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5681 651 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Umma Khan V"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6131 921h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6131 921h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.55,46.71&amp;year=1787">What was here in 1787? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
